Chapter: Simplify
  cites Foreword chapter MathLib
  cites Mathbook chapter Expr

 Algebraic simplification engine. Applies rewrite rules repeatedly
 until a fixed point (the expression stops changing). Rules include
 constant folding, identity elimination, commutativity normalization,
 power rules, log/exp cancellation, and trigonometric identities.
 Each rule is a pattern match on the expression tree.

Section: Constant Folding

 A pattern may not nest a constructor inside a constructor. Writing
 `is ExAdd (ExInt a) (ExInt b)` is a parse error, and the parenthesised
 form `is ExAdd (ExInt (a)) (ExInt (b))` is worse: it parses, binds
 nothing, and surfaces later as an unresolved call to `a`. So each rule
 set below matches the outer constructor once and hands the operands to
 a helper that takes them apart in the order the original arms were
 written. That order is the semantics -- the first matching arm wins --
 so it is preserved literally rather than tidied.

  fold-constants : Expr -> Expr
  fold-constants (e) = when e
    is ExAdd (x) (y) -> fc-add e x y
    is ExSub (x) (y) -> fc-sub e x y
    is ExMul (x) (y) -> fc-mul e x y
    is ExDiv (x) (y) -> fc-div e x y
    is ExPow (x) (y) -> fc-pow e x y
    is ExNeg (x) -> fc-neg e x
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-add : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-add (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExInt (a) -> when y
      is ExInt (b) -> ExInt (a + b)
      is ExRatio (n) (d) -> ex-ratio (a * d + n) d
      is otherwise -> e
    is ExRatio (n1) (d1) -> when y
      is ExRatio (n2) (d2) -> ex-ratio (n1 * d2 + n2 * d1) (d1 * d2)
      is ExInt (b) -> ex-ratio (n1 + b * d1) d1
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-sub : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-sub (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExInt (a) -> when y
      is ExInt (b) -> ExInt (a - b)
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-mul : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-mul (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExInt (a) -> when y
      is ExInt (b) -> ExInt (a * b)
      is ExRatio (n) (d) -> ex-ratio (a * n) d
      is otherwise -> e
    is ExRatio (n1) (d1) -> when y
      is ExRatio (n2) (d2) -> ex-ratio (n1 * n2) (d1 * d2)
      is ExInt (b) -> ex-ratio (n1 * b) d1
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-div : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-div (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExInt (a) -> when y
      is ExInt (b) -> if b == 0 then ExUndefined else ex-ratio a b
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-pow : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-pow (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExInt (a) -> when y
      is ExInt (b) -> if b >= 0 then ExInt (ex-int-pow a b) else ex-ratio 1 (ex-int-pow a (negate b))
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fc-neg : Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fc-neg (e) (x) = when x
    is ExInt (n) -> ExInt (negate n)
    is otherwise -> e

Section: Identity Rules

 Every identity rule tests an operand against a literal integer, so
 these read as an ordered if-chain over one predicate. The chain is in
 the same order the arms were, which matters: ExMul of -1 and 0 must
 give 0, because the zero rule was written above the negation rule.

  ex-is-int : Expr, Integer -> Boolean
  ex-is-int (x) (n) = when x
    is ExInt (v) -> v == n
    is otherwise -> False

  fold-identities : Expr -> Expr
  fold-identities (e) = when e
    is ExAdd (x) (y) -> if ex-is-int x 0 then y else if ex-is-int y 0 then x else e
    is ExSub (x) (y) -> if ex-is-int y 0 then x else e
    is ExMul (x) (y) -> fi-mul e x y
    is ExDiv (x) (y) -> if ex-is-int y 1 then x else if ex-is-int x 0 then ExInt 0 else e
    is ExPow (x) (y) -> fi-pow e x y
    is ExNeg (x) -> fi-neg e x
    is ExSqrt (x) -> fi-sqrt e x
    is otherwise -> e

  fi-mul : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fi-mul (e) (x) (y) =
    if ex-is-int x 0 then ExInt 0
    else if ex-is-int y 0 then ExInt 0
    else if ex-is-int x 1 then y
    else if ex-is-int y 1 then x
    else if ex-is-int x (-1) then ExNeg y
    else if ex-is-int y (-1) then ExNeg x
    else e

  fi-pow : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fi-pow (e) (x) (y) =
    if ex-is-int y 0 then ExInt 1
    else if ex-is-int y 1 then x
    else if ex-is-int x 0 then ExInt 0
    else if ex-is-int x 1 then ExInt 1
    else if ex-is-int y 2 then fi-pow-sqrt e x
    else e

  fi-pow-sqrt : Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fi-pow-sqrt (e) (x) = when x
    is ExSqrt (a) -> a
    is otherwise -> e

  fi-neg : Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fi-neg (e) (x) = when x
    is ExNeg (a) -> a
    is ExInt (0) -> ExInt 0
    is otherwise -> e

 sqrt of a perfect square folds to the root. The quadratic solver builds
 ExSqrt directly rather than through the ex-sqrt smart constructor, so
 without this rule `solve-quadratic 1 (-5) 6` prints
 `x = (5 + sqrt(1)) / 2 or x = (5 - sqrt(1)) / 2` instead of
 `x = 3 or x = 2`.

  fi-sqrt : Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fi-sqrt (e) (x) = when x
    is ExPow (b) (p) -> if ex-is-int p 2 then ExAbs b else e
    is ExInt (n) -> if ex-is-perfect-square n then ExInt (math-isqrt n) else e
    is otherwise -> e

Section: Algebraic Rules

 One behaviour change, deliberate and called out. The quotient power
 rule x^n / x^m = x^(n-m) was written as its own arm BELOW the general
 `ExDiv (a) (b)` equality arm, and that arm matches every ExDiv and
 answers `e` when the sides differ -- so the power rule could never
 fire. It is now reached from that arm's else branch, which is plainly
 what it was written to do. The product rule above it was always
 reachable and is unchanged.

  fold-algebraic : Expr -> Expr
  fold-algebraic (e) = when e
    is ExSub (x) (y) -> if ex-equal x y then ExInt 0 else e
    is ExAdd (x) (y) -> if ex-equal x y then ExMul (ExInt 2) x else e
    is ExMul (x) (y) -> fa-combine-powers e x y 1
    is ExDiv (x) (y) -> if ex-equal x y then ExInt 1 else fa-combine-powers e x y (-1)
    is ExPow (x) (y) -> fa-power-of-power e x y
    is otherwise -> e

  fa-combine-powers : Expr, Expr, Expr, Integer -> Expr
  fa-combine-powers (e) (x) (y) (sign) = when x
    is ExPow (b1) (p1) -> when y
      is ExPow (b2) (p2) -> fa-combine-exponents e b1 p1 b2 p2 sign
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fa-combine-exponents : Expr, Expr, Expr, Expr, Expr, Integer -> Expr
  fa-combine-exponents (e) (b1) (p1) (b2) (p2) (sign) = when p1
    is ExInt (n) -> when p2
      is ExInt (m) -> if ex-equal b1 b2 then ExPow b1 (ExInt (n + sign * m)) else e
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  fa-power-of-power : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  fa-power-of-power (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExPow (b) (p) -> when p
      is ExInt (n) -> when y
        is ExInt (m) -> ExPow b (ExInt (n * m))
        is otherwise -> e
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

Section: Function Rules

  fold-functions : Expr -> Expr
  fold-functions (e) = when e
    is ExFn ("ln") (args) ->
      if list-length args == 1 then ff-ln e (list-at args 0)
      else e
    is ExPow (x) (y) -> ff-exp-ln e x y
    is ExFn ("sin") (args) ->
      if list-length args == 1 then when list-at args 0
        is ExInt (0) -> ExInt 0
        is ExPi -> ExInt 0
        is otherwise -> e
      else e
    is ExFn ("cos") (args) ->
      if list-length args == 1 then when list-at args 0
        is ExInt (0) -> ExInt 1
        is ExPi -> ExInt (negate 1)
        is otherwise -> e
      else e
    is ExFn ("factorial") (args) ->
      if list-length args == 1 then when list-at args 0
        is ExInt (0) -> ExInt 1
        is ExInt (1) -> ExInt 1
        is ExInt (n) -> if n > 1 then ExInt (ex-factorial n) else e
        is otherwise -> e
      else e
    is otherwise -> e

  ff-ln : Expr, Expr -> Expr
  ff-ln (e) (arg) = when arg
    is ExE -> ExInt 1
    is ExInt (1) -> ExInt 0
    is ExPow (b) (x) -> when b
      is ExE -> x
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  ff-exp-ln : Expr, Expr, Expr -> Expr
  ff-exp-ln (e) (x) (y) = when x
    is ExE -> when y
      is ExFn (name) (args) ->
        if name == "ln" then (if list-length args == 1 then list-at args 0 else e)
        else e
      is otherwise -> e
    is otherwise -> e

  ex-factorial : Integer -> Integer
  ex-factorial (n) = if n <= 1 then 1 else n * ex-factorial (n - 1)

Section: Recursive Simplification

  simplify-children : Expr -> Expr
  simplify-children (e) = when e
    is ExAdd (a) (b) -> ExAdd (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExSub (a) (b) -> ExSub (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExMul (a) (b) -> ExMul (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExDiv (a) (b) -> ExDiv (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExPow (a) (b) -> ExPow (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExNeg (a) -> ExNeg (simplify a)
    is ExAbs (a) -> ExAbs (simplify a)
    is ExSqrt (a) -> ExSqrt (simplify a)
    is ExFn (name) (args) -> ExFn name (simplify-list args 0 (list-length args) [])
    is ExEq (a) (b) -> ExEq (simplify a) (simplify b)
    is ExDeriv (body) (var) -> ExDeriv (simplify body) var
    is ExIntegral (body) (var) -> ExIntegral (simplify body) var
    is otherwise -> e

  simplify-list : List Expr, Integer, Integer, List Expr -> List Expr
  simplify-list (exprs) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else simplify-list exprs (i + 1) n (list-push acc (simplify (list-at exprs i)))

Section: Main Simplify Loop

  simplify-step : Expr -> Expr
  simplify-step (e) =
    let e1 = simplify-children e
    in let e2 = fold-constants e1
    in let e3 = fold-identities e2
    in let e4 = fold-algebraic e3
    in fold-functions e4

 The rewrite loop is fuel-capped. It used to recurse until a step
 compared equal to its input, with nothing bounding it, so a rule that
 failed to converge -- or an ex-equal that could not recognise its own
 output, which is exactly what happened with ExFn -- ran forever on a
 machine with no GC and no scheduler to interrupt it. The cap is far
 above what any convergent expression needs, so it changes no answer
 that was previously returned; it only bounds one that was not.

  simplify-fuel : Integer = 1000

  simplify : Expr -> Expr
  simplify (e) = simplify-n e simplify-fuel

  simplify-n : Expr, Integer -> Expr
  simplify-n (e) (max-steps) =
    if max-steps <= 0 then e
    else let result = simplify-step e
    in if ex-equal result e then result
    else simplify-n result (max-steps - 1)

